{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghQasn3SSv9XyoCokwJH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral Co(II) I get **Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**. C can't be found from these bands.\n\n**Band assignment**\n\nA tetrahedral d⁷ ion has the same term pattern as an octahedral d³ ion: the ground term is ⁴A₂(F). The three spin-allowed bands are therefore:\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F) = 4 070 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) = 7 030 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) = 16 400 cm⁻¹\n\n**Equations used**\n- ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂ and ν₃ = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½·√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n- Adding these gives ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B.\n\n**Solving**\n1. From ν₁: 10Dq = 4 070, so **Dq = 407 cm⁻¹** (10Dq = Δt = 4 070 cm⁻¹).\n2. From the sum: 30Dq + 15B = 23 430. Since 30Dq = 12 210, 15B = 11 220, so **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Check**\n\nPutting Dq = 407 and B = 748 back into the full equation:\n- The square root is √(8.765×10⁷) ≈ 9 362.\n- The centre is 15Dq + 7.5B = 6 105 + 5 610 = 11 715.\n- This gives ν₂ ≈ 11 715 − 4 681 = 7 034 cm⁻¹ and ν₃ ≈ 11 715 + 4 681 = 16 396 cm⁻¹.\n\nBoth match the observed bands to within about 4 cm⁻¹, so the assignment is consistent. For comparison, the free-ion B for Co²⁺ is about 1 120 cm⁻¹. That puts β ≈ 0.67, which points to a fair amount of covalency.\n\n**Why C is missing**\n\nAll three bands are quartet → quartet transitions. Their energies depend only on Dq and B, so C cannot be found from them. You would need a spin-forbidden (doublet) band to get C.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from ν1=10Dq and B from ν2+ν3=30Dq+15B; C cannot be found from quartet-only bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":795,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":795,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"stand